  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 126 PM MDT Wed Jun 17 2026\n\nThe 17/12Z H500 hand analysis map is showing an anomalous\nblocking low sitting over the Hudson Bay region which is largely \nresponsible for driving the NOAM weather pattern attm. This \nincludes the tight NW flow gradient across the PacNW to the Central\nRockies...which is bringing the stronger gusts today. This \nstronger flow is also holding back a ridge over the \nIntermountain West under which a batch of moisture is trapped. \nThis pattern remains fairly static through the next 24-36 hours\nthough the strongest wind gradient aloft will shift slightly \nNorth and relax a bit over our CWA. This will reduce critical \nfire weather concerns to more a localized coverage before \nramping back up for the weekend. So less windy but no less dry \nand hot to end out the week with pockets of critical fire \nweather. The Hudson Bay low won't budge for the weekend but \nenergy in the E.Pacific will be strong enough to progress the \npattern through the mid and lower latitudes of the CONUS. This \nwill push the upstream ridge and moisture source across eastern \nGreat Basin and Rockies by Friday afternoon and introduce very \nremote chances of a mountain thunderstorm through the evening. \nAt this point wind and lightning will be more of a threat than \nmeasurable precipitation due to emaciated boundary layer \nmoisture thanks to the ongoing drought. Another windy/critical \nfire weather pattern sets up for Saturday as a wave digs through\nthe northern Great Basin and pushes a flattened ridge onto the \nPlains. The core of stronger winds at the base of this wave is \noverhead and with deep mixing...another breezy/windy...dry and \nhot day is expected with widespread critical fire weather a \nprobable outcome. Winds relax as we head into Sunday and early \nnext week with temperatures falling back a few degrees...however\ncritical fire weather remains a least localized. Precipitation \nchances remain meager even with forcing from this wave and a \ntrailing wave moving through on Sunday with interact with a \nmeandering surface boundary. Nothing to really hang a hat on yet\nbut likely at least some isolated thunderstorm chances. The \nupslope regime on the East side of the divide will have the \nbetter chances as usual.",
